 ## Background
-The canonical NeRF architecture, introduced by Mildenhall et al. in 2020 [Parent], established the paradigm of implicit neural representations for 3D scenes. Subsequent research rapidly expanded the family of specific model architectures to address various limitations, including computational efficiency [S1, unofficial], generalization to unseen scenes [S2], dynamic scene modeling [S1, unofficial], and single-view synthesis [S2]. The field has evolved from simple MLPs to complex hybrid systems involving Transformers [S3], diffusion models [S2], and explicit geometric priors [S3].
-
-## Sources
-[S1] NeRF的基础及后续扩展, https://pengsida.net/files/NeRF_Tutorial.pdf, fetched 2026-10-30 (unofficial)
+The canonical NeRF architecture [Parent] introduced the use of MLPs to represent continuous radiance fields. Subsequent research rapidly expanded this foundation to address specific challenges:
+*   **Generalization:** Methods like MultiPlaneNeRF [S1, unofficial], RING-NeRF [S1, unofficial], and Transformer-based architectures [S2, S3] aim to generalize across scenes rather than optimizing per-scene.
+*   **Efficiency:** Sparse and Efficient Representations [S1, unofficial], AdaNeRF [S1, unofficial], and MIMO-NeRF [S1, unofficial] focus on reducing computational cost and memory usage.
+*   **Single-View Synthesis:** SinNeRF [S2], NeRDi [S1, unofficial][S2], and DiffusioNeRF [S3] enable reconstruction from limited input data using priors.
+*   **Dynamic Scenes:** HexPlane [S1, unofficial], GNeuVox [S1, unofficial], and Ced-NeRF [S1, unofficial] handle temporal dynamics.
+*   **Generative Modeling:** pi-GAN [S1, unofficial], GIRAFFE [S1, unofficial], and Generative and Style-based Models [S1, unofficial] extend NeRF to generative tasks.
+*   **Physical Properties:** IRON [S1, unofficial], Appearance and Lighting Decomposition [S1, unofficial], and RelightingNeRF [S3] focus on materials and lighting.
